  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming trend through the rest of the week.\n\n-  Wildfire smoke will continue to impact the region until \n   further notice.\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure flattens heading into the weekend. Near-\n   critical to critical fire weather expected due to hot, dry and\n   breezy conditions. \n\nA ridge of high pressure will continue to build over the western \nUnited States, maintaining warm, dry, and stable conditions \nacross western Montana and north central Idaho. Temperatures will \nwarm significantly by Friday and Saturday, with lower elevations \nclimbing back into the 90s and localized areas exceeding 100 \ndegrees. As the upper ridge begins to flatten and transition into \na more zonal flow this weekend, afternoon surface winds will \nincrease. The combination of high temperatures, low relative \nhumidity, breezy afternoon winds, and critically low fuel moisture\nwill maintain near-critical fire weather conditions, likely \npeaking in intensity from Friday into Saturday.\n\nWildfire smoke will continue to be a forecast challenge across \nthe region in terms of how much air quality is degraded. Strong \nradiational cooling under cloud-free, albeit smoky, skies will \npromote the development of sharp nocturnal valley inversions each \nmorning. These inversions will trap particulate matter near the \nsurface, degrading air quality and visibility during the morning \nhours. Afternoon diurnal heating will promote boundary layer \nmixing, which will temporarily aid in dispersing near-surface \nsmoke. However, this increased ventilation will also invigorate \nregional wildfire activity, potentially generating thicker smoke \nplumes aloft. Current high-resolution smoke modeling indicates \nnear-surface smoke concentrations will peak early this afternoon \nbefore modest improvement occurs with peak heating by early \nevening.\n\nA subtle increase in mid-level moisture tracking along the periphery \nof the upper ridge may introduce a slight chance for high-based \nconvection from Friday through the weekend, particularly across \nsouthwest Montana. The anomalously dry sub-cloud layer will pose a\nsignificant risk for localized, erratic convective wind gusts of \n40 to 55 mph, alongside the threat of new lightning ignitions.\n\nHeading into next week, forecast confidence remains low regarding \na gradual increase in atmospheric moisture. A substantial \nsynoptic pattern shift, such as the arrival of a deep Pacific \nNorthwest upper-level trough, will ultimately be required to \nsignificantly diminish the prevailing fire weather threat and \nalleviate the persistent smoke. Current model runs show a 20 to 30\npercent chance of this occurring by mid to late next week.",
